1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,202
A repository has worked for
weeks. git status is clean.

2
00:00:08,242 --> 00:00:13,917
git log --oneline prints the history. Then
one day somebody runs git log --stat, and

3
00:00:13,957 --> 00:00:18,340
Git stops with an error about a
data stream and a corrupt object.

4
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:25,182
The engineer's first move is the one
almost everyone makes: git fetch, which

5
00:00:25,222 --> 00:00:32,412
downloads the objects the server has and
you lack. It exits with status 0 and prints

6
00:00:32,452 --> 00:00:39,556
nothing. The error is still there. The
second move is to delete the clone and start

7
00:00:39,596 --> 00:00:44,807
over. Fine if everything was
pushed, and a loss if it wasn't.

8
00:00:45,147 --> 00:00:50,290
Your CTO's question is short: "Is the
repository damaged, how badly, and can you fix

9
00:00:50,330 --> 00:00:55,349
it without losing what is only on that
machine?" To answer, you need to know why a

10
00:00:55,389 --> 00:00:59,175
fetch doesn't help, and what
does. Remember that silent fetch.

11
00:01:01,015 --> 00:01:06,268
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:01:06,308 --> 00:01:13,557
Every recovery so far was about a lost name.
The object existed, and you had to find

13
00:01:13,597 --> 00:01:20,325
it and point a ref at it. An object is
one stored unit, named by a hash of its

14
00:01:20,365 --> 00:01:24,577
content. A ref is a name
that holds an object ID.

15
00:01:24,917 --> 00:01:34,163
Today is the other kind of problem: an
object that's still needed can't be read.

16
00:01:34,203 --> 00:01:43,217
A reachable commit, tree or blob, one
that a ref still leads to, is missing or

17
00:01:43,257 --> 00:01:44,146
corrupt.

18
00:01:44,486 --> 00:01:49,775
The causes are outside Git: failing
hardware, and tools that copy the repository

19
00:01:49,815 --> 00:01:53,306
file by file. The Git FAQ
is direct about the second.

20
00:01:53,346 --> 00:01:58,836
It says not to use a cloud syncing service
to sync any portion of a Git repository,

21
00:01:58,876 --> 00:02:04,298
"since this can cause corruption, such as
missing objects, changed or added files,

22
00:02:04,338 --> 00:02:05,165
broken refs".

23
00:02:05,504 --> 00:02:10,332
In the second half you do something a senior
engineer must be able to do: list what

24
00:02:10,372 --> 00:02:12,971
can't be recovered, with
the reason for each.

25
00:02:14,811 --> 00:02:20,028
recognize object corruption from the messages
of ordinary commands and of git fsck;

26
00:02:20,368 --> 00:02:24,501
explain why git fetch does not replace
a corrupt object, and what does;

27
00:02:24,841 --> 00:02:28,138
repair a repository with one
missing object from another clone;

28
00:02:28,478 --> 00:02:31,518
list what Git cannot recover
and give the reason for each.

29
00:02:33,358 --> 00:02:37,807
The symptom. Commands that need the object
fail. Commands that don't need it keep

30
00:02:37,847 --> 00:02:40,910
working, which is why damage
can go unnoticed for weeks.

31
00:02:40,950 --> 00:02:45,288
git status and git log --oneline read
commits and the index, your proposed next

32
00:02:45,328 --> 00:02:49,887
commit. git log --stat needs trees and
blobs: directory listings and file contents.

33
00:02:50,227 --> 00:02:53,648
The diagnosis. git fsck verifies that
everything reachable is present and well

34
00:02:53,688 --> 00:02:57,198
formed, and its words for damage differ
from the harmless reports of the earlier

35
00:02:57,238 --> 00:03:00,703
videos. dangling is information. Lines
that begin with missing, broken or error

36
00:03:00,743 --> 00:03:04,430
describe a repository that needs repair. A
file that's present but unreadable counts

37
00:03:04,470 --> 00:03:04,918
as missing.

38
00:03:05,258 --> 00:03:08,565
Why a fetch does not repair it.
A fetch negotiates by commits.

39
00:03:08,605 --> 00:03:13,099
Your repository says which commits it has,
and the server sends what isn't reachable

40
00:03:13,139 --> 00:03:17,579
from them. Now the names: main and origin/main,
your record of the server's branch,

41
00:03:17,619 --> 00:03:20,709
sit on the same commit. Your
refs say you have everything.

42
00:03:20,749 --> 00:03:22,221
So the server sends nothing.

43
00:03:22,561 --> 00:03:26,554
What does repair it. An object's
ID is determined by its content.

44
00:03:26,594 --> 00:03:30,773
So the same object from any other
repository is the object you lost.

45
00:03:30,813 --> 00:03:32,076
There are two routes.

46
00:03:32,416 --> 00:03:37,242
The precise route: ask a healthy clone
for a pack that contains the object, and

47
00:03:37,282 --> 00:03:42,231
unpack it in the damaged repository. git
pack-objects reads IDs on standard input

48
00:03:42,271 --> 00:03:45,495
and writes a pack, one file
that stores many objects.

49
00:03:45,535 --> 00:03:49,375
git unpack-objects stores the
objects of a pack as loose files.

50
00:03:49,715 --> 00:03:57,499
One detail decides whether it works. git
unpack-objects doesn't write objects that

51
00:03:57,539 --> 00:04:04,559
already exist in the repository, and
a damaged file with that name exists.

52
00:04:04,599 --> 00:04:12,191
Move it out first. Move it, don't delete
it: the official how-to on this subject

53
00:04:12,231 --> 00:04:20,110
asks you to keep it, since a corrupt object
can only be analysed next to its intact

54
00:04:20,150 --> 00:04:20,587
twin.

55
00:04:20,927 --> 00:04:27,670
The blunt route: git fetch --refetch,
which in the manual's words "fetches all

56
00:04:27,710 --> 00:04:34,540
objects as a fresh clone would". The
release notes of Git 2.36, which added it,

57
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:41,324
describe it as useful "when you cannot
trust what you have in the local object

58
00:04:41,364 --> 00:04:41,932
store".

59
00:04:42,272 --> 00:04:47,497
The limit. --refetch can only
bring back what the server has.

60
00:04:47,537 --> 00:04:54,661
Commits that exist only in your clone and
lost an object are repaired from your own

61
00:04:54,701 --> 00:05:01,738
working tree, the files you edit, if the
file is still there, with git hash-object

62
00:05:01,778 --> 00:05:06,658
-w. Otherwise they're lost
from that point of history on.

63
00:05:06,998 --> 00:05:11,993
A corrupt index is the mild case. Every
command that looks at the working tree fails

64
00:05:12,033 --> 00:05:16,489
with "bad signature" and "index file
corrupt". History commands still work.

65
00:05:16,529 --> 00:05:21,465
The index is derived data, except for one
thing: the record of what you had staged.

66
00:05:21,505 --> 00:05:26,441
Move the file away, and git reset builds a
new index from HEAD without touching the

67
00:05:26,481 --> 00:05:31,177
working tree. Your edits are still in the
files. What's gone is the distinction

68
00:05:31,217 --> 00:05:32,855
between staged and unstaged.

69
00:05:33,195 --> 00:05:38,205
When not to repair in place:
when the repair is doubtful.

70
00:05:38,245 --> 00:05:45,026
Section 13.17 says to copy the whole
repository directory first, while no Git

71
00:05:45,066 --> 00:05:51,847
command runs, and work on the copy. And
keep repositories out of file-syncing

72
00:05:51,887 --> 00:05:54,593
folders. That's the prevention.

73
00:05:56,433 --> 00:06:01,246
In the vault, boxes are objects, index
cards are refs, the ledger is the reflog.

74
00:06:01,286 --> 00:06:06,159
Every earlier accident was a misplaced
index card. Today a box itself is damaged.

75
00:06:06,199 --> 00:06:10,950
The card still points at it, the ledger
still mentions it, and when you open it

76
00:06:10,990 --> 00:06:12,345
there's garbage inside.

77
00:06:12,685 --> 00:06:17,222
The auditor, git fsck, reports that
differently. An unmentioned box is "dangling".

78
00:06:17,262 --> 00:06:19,901
A mentioned box that can't
be read is "missing".

79
00:06:20,241 --> 00:06:23,436
Why doesn't the other branch office
send a replacement when you visit?

80
00:06:23,476 --> 00:06:26,671
Because your visit, the fetch, begins
with you reading out your cards.

81
00:06:26,711 --> 00:06:30,045
Every card you hold is fine. The other
office concludes you need nothing.

82
00:06:30,085 --> 00:06:31,246
There's your silent fetch.

83
00:06:31,586 --> 00:06:40,136
The useful property is the one that makes
Git what it is: a box number is computed

84
00:06:40,176 --> 00:06:48,096
from the contents. A box with the same
number from any office holds the same

85
00:06:48,136 --> 00:06:55,848
contents, byte for byte. The picture
breaks only at the door of the vault.

86
00:06:55,888 --> 00:07:03,075
A box that no other office ever
received can't be replaced by anyone.

87
00:07:04,915 --> 00:07:07,433
On top, main leads to commit
688c9f2, which needs tree 3a87d97.

88
00:07:07,473 --> 00:07:10,680
A ref reaches it: damage. Below, a commit
nothing reaches: dangling, information

89
00:07:10,720 --> 00:07:10,883
only.

90
00:07:11,223 --> 00:07:19,579
Try it now, on paper. Pause me for thirty
seconds and write your answer: three

91
00:07:19,619 --> 00:07:24,745
things Git can't bring
back, each with a reason.

92
00:07:29,585 --> 00:07:34,277
Check your three against the right column:
no object was ever written, or the object

93
00:07:34,317 --> 00:07:35,573
and its names are gone.

94
00:07:35,913 --> 00:07:38,850
Two losses that people expect
in this table aren't in it.

95
00:07:38,890 --> 00:07:42,767
A commit removed by a rebase, an amend
or a reset is recoverable for weeks.

96
00:07:42,807 --> 00:07:46,528
And a deleted branch is recoverable
for as long as you can find its tip.

97
00:07:48,368 --> 00:07:53,268
The tree of the second-newest commit,
3a87d97, is now a file of seven bytes: a

98
00:07:53,308 --> 00:07:58,209
simulated bad disk block. Predict which
of the next three commands still work.

99
00:07:58,249 --> 00:07:59,920
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

100
00:08:04,760 --> 00:08:10,715
Status and the one-line log work:
they read commits and the index.

101
00:08:10,755 --> 00:08:16,257
git log --stat needs the tree
of the second commit and stops.

102
00:08:16,297 --> 00:08:22,435
Read the last line: it names the
object and the file it's stored in.

103
00:08:22,775 --> 00:08:26,199
Exit status 3, and the line
that matters: "missing tree".

104
00:08:26,239 --> 00:08:29,725
That's the diagnosis. Now the
reflex, a fetch. Quick quiz.

105
00:08:29,765 --> 00:08:34,405
One: it downloads the tree. Two: an
error. Three: nothing, and exit status 0.

106
00:08:34,445 --> 00:08:35,377
Say it out loud.

107
00:08:40,217 --> 00:08:45,044
Option three. Exit status 0, nothing
transferred, and the tree is still missing.

108
00:08:45,084 --> 00:08:49,424
The negotiation was by commits, and
your refs say you have every commit.

109
00:08:49,764 --> 00:08:54,511
Now the precise repair. A colleague's clone,
in the directory asha, has the object.

110
00:08:56,851 --> 00:09:09,034
Watch this in two attempts. The first
one fails, and the reason is the lesson.

111
00:09:09,374 --> 00:09:14,766
After the first attempt nothing changed: a
file with that name exists, so the object

112
00:09:14,806 --> 00:09:19,940
wasn't written. The damaged file is moved
out of the object database and kept as

113
00:09:19,980 --> 00:09:25,243
evidence. The second attempt writes the
object, git fsck exits with 0, and git log

114
00:09:25,283 --> 00:09:26,472
--stat works again.

115
00:09:26,812 --> 00:09:33,480
Moving a file out of .git/objects by
hand is outside Git's own commands.

116
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:41,306
The chapter's safety table gives the rule
for such steps: move the file aside, don't

117
00:09:41,346 --> 00:09:45,685
delete it, so that recovery
is to move it back.

118
00:09:46,025 --> 00:09:51,058
The blunt repair, for when many objects are
affected or you can't reach a teammate's

119
00:09:51,098 --> 00:09:55,286
disk. The script removes two objects,
then downloads everything again.

120
00:09:55,626 --> 00:10:01,292
Before: a "broken link" from commit
688c9f2 to the tree, a missing blob and a

121
00:10:01,332 --> 00:10:07,370
missing tree. After --refetch: clean.
Remember the limit: it brings back only what

122
00:10:07,410 --> 00:10:08,481
the server has.

123
00:10:10,321 --> 00:10:11,969
Five mistakes to watch for.

124
00:10:12,309 --> 00:10:15,765
Running git fetch and expecting
it to heal the repository.

125
00:10:15,805 --> 00:10:20,827
Root cause: a fetch negotiates by commits,
and the refs say every commit is present,

126
00:10:20,867 --> 00:10:22,273
so nothing is requested.

127
00:10:22,613 --> 00:10:27,122
Unpacking a good copy over the damaged file.
Root cause: git unpack-objects does not

128
00:10:27,162 --> 00:10:31,672
write an object that already exists in the
repository, and the damaged file has that

129
00:10:31,712 --> 00:10:31,942
name.

130
00:10:32,282 --> 00:10:36,461
Deleting the clone as the first step.
Root cause: the clone may hold commits,

131
00:10:36,501 --> 00:10:41,008
stashes and reflogs that exist nowhere else;
a fresh clone has only what the server

132
00:10:41,048 --> 00:10:41,227
has.

133
00:10:41,567 --> 00:10:45,970
Treating dangling lines as corruption.
Root cause: dangling means unreachable and

134
00:10:46,010 --> 00:10:49,646
unreferenced; only missing, broken
and error lines describe damage.

135
00:10:49,986 --> 00:10:55,297
Keeping a repository in a file-syncing
folder. Root cause: such tools copy files one

136
00:10:55,337 --> 00:11:00,584
by one without Git's locking, which the Git
FAQ names as a cause of missing objects

137
00:11:00,624 --> 00:11:01,603
and broken refs.

138
00:11:03,443 --> 00:11:08,315
Now, out of the lab. An ML engineer
keeps a training repository inside a

139
00:11:08,355 --> 00:11:13,091
cloud-synced folder so that a notebook
server and a laptop "share" it.

140
00:11:13,131 --> 00:11:18,616
After a crash of the sync client, git diff
against last week fails with a corrupt

141
00:11:18,656 --> 00:11:23,733
loose object. git status still works,
which is why nobody noticed for days.

142
00:11:24,073 --> 00:11:28,765
The lead's procedure follows the chapter.
Copy the whole directory first, with no

143
00:11:28,805 --> 00:11:33,498
Git command running, and work on the copy.
Run git fsck and read only the missing

144
00:11:33,538 --> 00:11:37,997
and broken lines: two blobs and a tree.
All three belong to commits that were

145
00:11:38,037 --> 00:11:42,788
pushed, so git fetch --refetch from the
server repairs them, and git fsck is clean

146
00:11:42,828 --> 00:11:43,431
afterwards.

147
00:11:43,771 --> 00:11:49,601
One more commit existed only locally. Its
files are still in the working tree, so

148
00:11:49,641 --> 00:11:55,617
nothing of it was lost. The repository then
moves out of the synced folder, and the

149
00:11:55,657 --> 00:12:00,835
two machines share work the way Git
intends: through pushes and fetches.

150
00:12:01,175 --> 00:12:04,685
The statement to the CTO is exact: what was
damaged, what repaired it, what existed

151
00:12:04,725 --> 00:12:07,892
only on that machine, and what was
changed so that it doesn't happen again.

152
00:12:09,732 --> 00:12:14,421
Your turn. Do Exercise 12.8, Level 3,
"The backup that will not clone", in

153
00:12:14,461 --> 00:12:17,232
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

154
00:12:17,572 --> 00:12:22,815
Before you run anything, predict which
commands will still succeed on the damaged

155
00:12:22,855 --> 00:12:27,445
repository and which will fail, and
say which objects each has to read.

156
00:12:27,485 --> 00:12:32,923
Then decide which of the two repairs applies,
and predict what git fsck prints after

157
00:12:32,963 --> 00:12:33,575
each step.

158
00:12:33,915 --> 00:12:39,519
The challenge is Exercise 12.12, Level 5,
"Power loss in the middle of a commit", in

159
00:12:39,559 --> 00:12:40,459
the same file.

160
00:12:42,299 --> 00:12:43,270
Answer out loud.

161
00:12:48,110 --> 00:12:52,612
A strong answer explains the fetch negotiation
in a sentence or two: what the client

162
00:12:52,652 --> 00:12:55,803
tells the server, and why the
server finds nothing to send.

163
00:12:55,843 --> 00:13:00,183
It uses content addressing to justify
taking the object from elsewhere, and gives

164
00:13:00,223 --> 00:13:04,454
two repairs with their trade-off. It
mentions the detail that makes the precise

165
00:13:04,494 --> 00:13:08,943
repair fail the first time. And it states
the limit for history that exists only in

166
00:13:08,983 --> 00:13:09,916
the damaged clone.

167
00:13:11,756 --> 00:13:14,683
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

168
00:13:15,023 --> 00:13:20,432
Damage is an object that a ref still reaches
and that cannot be read; git fsck calls

169
00:13:20,472 --> 00:13:22,831
it missing or broken, never dangling.

170
00:13:23,171 --> 00:13:27,972
A fetch does not repair it, because the
negotiation is by commits and my refs say I

171
00:13:28,012 --> 00:13:28,788
have them all.

172
00:13:29,128 --> 00:13:33,395
The same object from any repository is
the same bytes; I can unpack it from a

173
00:13:33,435 --> 00:13:37,479
healthy clone after moving the damaged
file aside, or refetch everything.

174
00:13:37,819 --> 00:13:42,856
A corrupt index is rebuilt with git reset;
only the record of what was staged is

175
00:13:42,896 --> 00:13:43,173
lost.

176
00:13:43,513 --> 00:13:47,522
I can list what Git cannot recover, and for
each item say that no object was written

177
00:13:47,562 --> 00:13:49,547
or that the object and its names are gone.

178
00:13:51,387 --> 00:13:56,157
Read sections 13.11 and 13.12 of Chapter
13. Then write the CTO statement that Q199

179
00:13:56,197 --> 00:13:57,896
of the question bank asks for.

180
00:13:58,236 --> 00:14:01,124
Today you diagnosed real damage
and repaired it two ways.

181
00:14:01,164 --> 00:14:05,388
Replay the corruption lab before the next
video. Next time: the point of no return,

182
00:14:05,428 --> 00:14:09,653
backup refs, bundles, and what GitHub adds.
Until then, look at the state first and

183
00:14:09,693 --> 00:14:11,553
type second. See you in the next one.
